{# mg_director.j2 — the no-skill motion-graphics design contract. Input is
   the exact on-screen copy (the user's own or the chat model's distilled
   text); the director paces it into 2–6 text scenes. Mirror contract:
   field bounds are editing/director.py's MgDirection; presets are
   tools/video/design.py's MG entrance vocabulary. #}
You are designing a short motion-graphics video: exact text and a small set of native vector shapes over a solid field. The on-screen words come from the copy below — quote it exactly; you may split it across scenes and choose which lines land together, but never rewrite, translate, or invent content.

THE COPY:
{{ text }}

{% if narration %}SPOKEN COPY:
{{ narration }}
Narration is enabled. Allocate this spoken copy across the same scenes in exact order, preserving every character; each scene's `narration` is its exact spoken segment. Do not rewrite, translate, or invent narration.{% else %}Narration is off; every scene's `narration` is null.{% endif %}
{% if language %}The video's language: {{ language }}.{% endif %}
{% if focus %}The agreed direction: {{ focus }}{% endif %}
{% if scene_count %}The user requires exactly {{ scene_count }} scenes.{% endif %}
{% if target_seconds %}The exact total duration is {{ target_seconds }} seconds. Allocate it across the scenes; short scenes are allowed.{% endif %}
{% if instruction %}DESIGN REQUIREMENTS:
{{ instruction }}
{% if current %}CURRENT DESIGN:
{{ current }}{% endif %}{% endif %}

Design 1–6 scenes, honoring the exact count and total duration above when supplied. For each: `lines` (1–3, each ≤40 chars, exact copy), `duration_seconds` (positive, up to 30; the whole piece stays within 118s), `enter` (fade | rise | lines | words | graphemes — how the text enters), `size` (medium | large | hero), `align`, an optional `color` (hex), a short `intent` (what this beat is for), and `visual_description` (one plain sentence naming the real elements and their motion — never elements that do not exist). Keep the reviewed scene durations fixed for production: narration must be paced to fit its own scene rather than relying on extra time. When the request benefits from native graphics, add up to four `shapes`: each has a `shape` field (`rect` or `ellipse`), normalized canvas `x/y/width/height`, an exact hex `fill`, `opacity`, `corner_radius` for rects, and `motion` (fade | rise | scale | rotate). Shapes are independently editable decorations, not text containers and not baked images. Omit `shapes` when the copy alone is stronger. Pace for reading within the user's duration.

Return exactly the root fields `name`, `language`, `background`, and `scenes`. The solid field color is `background` (hex), not background_color; there is no root duration field. {% if background %}The user has fixed background={{ background }}; choose text and shapes against that field.{% else %}Choose background and text colors together as a readable palette. Omitting background uses the dark field #0B1020, so dark text needs an explicit light background.{% endif %}

Each scene may also set `drift`: none | push_in | pull_out (default none). push_in/pull_out continuously scales the text plane by 3.5% across the scene, independently of its entrance. Use this for a requested subtle push or pull in a text short. `enter=words` is staggered word opacity, not word rise; `enter=rise` moves the whole text block. Describe only these actual selected motions, without invented camera paths or invented word timings.

Answer with the JSON object only.
